//? Title: Beam-Stack Search
//? Subtitle: Making beam search complete and optimal by backtracking over f-ranges, its memory–time trade-off, and why it needs to remember finished cells, on the Maunga Whau elevation map with datamunge's algorithms::terrain_beam_stack_search
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Beam search bounds memory by keeping the best \(B\) nodes per layer, but it can miss the goal or
return a poor path. Beam-stack search keeps the same beam and stores a range of \(f\)-values for
each layer on a stack. When it reaches a dead end it backtracks, regenerating the deepest layer
with the next slice of that range. We prove that this makes the search complete and optimal with
at most \(B\) nodes per layer. On the Maunga Whau elevation map, every beam width finds and
proves the optimal route, from \(B=1\), holding 121 nodes, upwards. The proof hides a cost: once a
layer is dropped its cells are forgotten. Without a table of finished cells, widths up to 16
were still running after \(10^7\) expansions, with one cell expanded 83,392 times. With the
table every width finishes within 205,000 expansions and 21 ms on this machine.
)

\toc

> The method

>> From beam search to beam-stack search

Beam search keeps the best \(B\) nodes of each layer and forgets the rest. The beam search
report showed what that costs on a terrain map: with \(B\le2\) it found no path at all, and
with \(B=4\) or 8 it returned a route about 25% too dear. Beam-stack search (Zhou and Hansen
2005) keeps the beam's memory bound but remembers *which part* of each layer it has explored,
so that it can come back for the rest.

Write \(f(v)=g(v)+h(v)\), with \(h\) admissible, and order the candidates of a layer by the key
\((f(v),v)\), using the cell index to break ties so the order is total. Each stored layer \(l\)
carries a half-open range \([\ell_l,u_l)\) of keys on a stack, starting at
\([-\infty,\infty)\). Layer \(l+1\) is built from layer \(l\) exactly as in beam search, with
three filters: only children whose key lies in \([\ell_{l+1},u_{l+1})\) are kept, children with
\(f\ge U\) (the best goal cost so far) are pruned, and a child is dropped if its cell already sits
in a stored layer with no greater \(g\). If more than \(B\) children survive, the best \(B\) are
kept and \(u_{l+1}\) is set to the key of the first one left out. The search goes deeper until a
layer comes out empty. Then it *backtracks*:

```
while the stack has layers below the start:
    top <- deepest layer
    if u_top = infinity or u_top.f >= U: drop top            # every slice explored
    else:
        replace top by the slice [u_top, infinity) regenerated from the layer above
        if that slice is non-empty: resume going deeper
```

Each layer is thus explored in consecutive slices \([-\infty,u^{(1)}),[u^{(1)},u^{(2)}),\dots\), at
most \(B\) nodes at a time, in increasing order of \(f\).

>> Completeness and optimality

The slices of a layer partition its candidates. The candidate set of layer \(l+1\) depends only on
the stored layers \(0,\dots,l\) and on \(U\), and neither of those changes while layer \(l+1\) is
being sliced, except that \(U\) can fall. A fall in \(U\) only prunes candidates that could not
improve on it. So when layer \(l+1\) is dropped, every child of the current slice of layer \(l\)
with \(f<U\) has been placed in some slice, and by induction from the deepest layer upwards, every
extension of it has been explored or pruned.

The pruning is safe. Admissibility gives \(g^*(v)+h(v)\le C^*\) along an optimal path, so no prefix
of an optimal path is pruned by the bound while \(U>C^*\). A child dropped as a duplicate has a
copy in a stored layer with \(g\) no greater. Every path through the dropped child can be
re-routed through the copy at no greater cost, and the copy's extensions are being explored. When
the stack empties, every path cheaper than \(U\) has therefore been ruled out, and

$$
U=C^*\quad\text{(if a path exists)}.
$$
\alttext(U equals C star, if a path exists.)

The memory is at most \(B\) nodes per stored layer. With \(U\) finite and every step costing at
least \(c_{\min}\) (one cell, 10 m here), no layer deeper than \(U/c_{\min}\) can hold a node
with \(f<U\), so

$$
\text{stored nodes}\;\le\;B\left(\frac{U}{c_{\min}}+1\right).
$$
\alttext(Stored nodes are at most B times the quantity U over c min plus 1.)

>> The catch: forgotten cells

The price of the memory bound is time. When a layer is dropped, its cells are forgotten, so the
duplicate check no longer sees them. On a graph with many routes of similar cost, a grid most of
all, the same cell can then be reached and re-explored along exponentially many paths, one for
each combination of slices above it.

`algorithms::terrain_beam_stack_search()` therefore has an option, `remember_finished`. When a
layer is dropped, every node in it is *finished*: all its extensions with \(f<U\) have been
explored, and \(U\) only falls. The smallest such \(g\) per cell goes into a table \(F\), and a later
node \(v\) with \(g\ge F(v)\) is pruned. It is dominated by the finished copy, whose extensions
were already covered. This keeps the result optimal and removes the re-expansion, but the
table holds one number per cell, so the memory is no longer bounded by \(B\).

> The terrain

As in the A* and beam search reports, `dm::datasets::volcano()` gives the topography of Maunga
Whau, Auckland, on an \(87\times61\) grid with 10 m spacing. A step costs its horizontal length plus
\(\lambda=5000/600\approx8.3\) per metre climbed. The walker goes from the south-east foot to the
north-west foot. The heuristic is octile distance plus \(\lambda\) times the net height still to
gain. A* gives the optimum \(C^*=1291.5\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/beam_stack_anytime.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto grid = alg::terrain_from_frame(dm::datasets::volcano(), "x", "y", "elevation", 5000.0 / 600.0);
  const std::size_t start = 80 * grid.cols + 5, goal = 5 * grid.cols + 55;
  const auto kind = alg::TerrainHeuristic::OctileClimb;
  const auto optimal = alg::terrain_a_star(grid, start, goal, kind);
  const double inf = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  const std::size_t budget = 10'000'000;
  std::print("A*: cost {:.1f}, {} cells expanded, {} queue pushes.\n\n", optimal.cost,
             optimal.expansion_order.size(), optimal.pushes);

  std::print("| \\(B\\) | finished-cell table | proved optimal | cost | first path | expansions | backtracks "
             "| largest store | depth | time (ms) |\n| ---: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> mem_table, exp_table, mem_plain, exp_plain;
  std::vector<std::string> label_table;
  for (const bool table : {true, false}) {
    for (const std::size_t width : {1UL, 2UL, 4UL, 8UL, 16UL, 32UL, 64UL, 128UL}) {
      const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
      const auto r = alg::terrain_beam_stack_search(grid, start, goal, kind, width, inf, table, budget);
      const double ms = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.1f} |\n", width, table ? "yes" : "no",
                 r.exhausted ? "yes" : "no (budget)", r.best.cost, r.incumbent_costs.front(),
                 r.best.expansion_order.size(), r.backtracks, r.max_stored, r.max_depth, ms);
      if (r.exhausted) {
        (table ? mem_table : mem_plain).push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(r.max_stored)));
        (table ? exp_table : exp_plain).push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(r.best.expansion_order.size())));
        if (table) label_table.push_back(std::format("B={}", width));
      }
    }
  }

  // Anytime behaviour: best cost so far against expansions, with the finished-cell table.
  auto anytime = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, dm::RGB>> widths = {
      {1, {220, 38, 38}}, {4, {217, 119, 6}}, {16, {22, 163, 74}}, {64, {37, 99, 235}}};
  for (const auto& [width, colour] : widths) {
    const auto r = alg::terrain_beam_stack_search(grid, start, goal, kind, width, inf, true);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < r.incumbent_costs.size(); ++k) {
      if (k > 0) {
        x.push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(r.incumbent_expansions[k])));
        y.push_back(r.incumbent_costs[k - 1] / optimal.cost);
      }
      x.push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(r.incumbent_expansions[k])));
      y.push_back(r.incumbent_costs[k] / optimal.cost);
    }
    x.push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(r.best.expansion_order.size())));
    y.push_back(r.best.cost / optimal.cost);
    anytime.lines(x, y, std::format("B = {}", width), colour, 2.0).points({x.back()}, {y.back()}, "", colour, 7.0);
  }
  anytime.abline_h(1.0, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
      .y_limits(0.95, 1.75)
      .title("Best path so far (dot: search finished)")
      .x_label("log10 expansions")
      .y_label("cost / C*");

  auto trade = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                   .lines(exp_table, mem_table, "with table", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
                   .points(exp_table, mem_table, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
                   .points(exp_plain, mem_plain, "without table", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 9.0)
                   .points({std::log10(static_cast<double>(optimal.expansion_order.size()))},
                           {std::log10(static_cast<double>(optimal.pushes))}, "A* (queue pushes)",
                           dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 8.0)
                   .x_limits(3.0, 5.6)
                   .y_limits(1.9, 4.4)
                   .title("Nodes held against work (finished searches)")
                   .x_label("log10 expansions")
                   .y_label("log10 largest number of stored nodes");
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < label_table.size(); ++k)
    trade.text(exp_table[k] + 0.03, mem_table[k] - 0.1, label_table[k], dm::RGB{30, 64, 175}, 10.0);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(anytime).add(trade).size(1200, 470).save_svg("build/beam_stack_anytime.svg");
}
```
