// result_end

\caption(Left: the cost of the best path found so far, relative to the optimum, against the number of expansions, for beam-stack search with the finished-cell table; each line ends with a dot when the search has finished and proved its path optimal. Right: largest number of nodes stored in the beam layers against expansions, for every search that finished, and for A*.)
\alttext(Left: step lines of cost relative to the optimum against log10 expansions. B equals 1 (red) starts at 1.28 near 10 to the 2.1 and steps down slowly, reaching 1 and finishing at about 10 to the 5.2. B equals 4 (orange) starts higher, at 1.54 near 10 to the 2.7, stays above 1.27 until about 10 to the 4.6, and finishes at about 10 to the 5.3. B equals 16 (green) and B equals 64 (blue) appear only as short ticks just above 1, finishing at about 10 to the 3.9 and 10 to the 3.7. Right: log10 largest store against log10 expansions. The blue points with the table are labelled by width: B equals 1 at (5.19, 2.08), B equals 2 at (4.92, 2.32), B equals 4 at (5.31, 2.75), B equals 8 at (4.35, 2.89), B equals 16 at (3.91, 3.19), B equals 32 at (3.62, 3.48), B equals 64 at (3.74, 3.74), B equals 128 at (3.91, 3.91). Orange points without the table exist only for B equals 32, at (3.91, 3.48), and for 64 and 128, on top of the blue ones. A black A* point sits at (3.24, 3.55).)

With the finished-cell table, every width from 1 to 128 ends with the optimal cost 1291.5 and a
proof that it is optimal. The first path is often much worse: 28% above the optimum for
\(B=1\) and 54% for \(B=4\). The anytime curves then show the bound \(U\) creeping down as
backtracking tries slice after slice. With \(B=1\), the search holds at most 121 nodes in its
layers, one per layer down to depth 120, within the bound \(B(U/c_{\min}+1)\approx166\) from
the first path. It needs 155,657 expansions and 31,894 backtracks, about 16 ms on this
machine. Width 4 is slower than width 1 (204,666 expansions), because its first path was worse
and took the search down a deep southern detour (depth 142). From \(B=8\) the work falls
quickly. It is smallest at \(B=32\), with 4,167 expansions, 27 backtracks and 3,003 stored
nodes. At \(B=64\) and 128 the beam is never cut below \(U\), so there are no backtracks, and
the search is plain beam search with a bound. A* expands only 1,732 cells, but it keeps a
queue of up to 3,571 entries and a cost for every cell it touches.

The right panel is the trade-off: the narrower the beam, the less the layers hold, and the
more the search has to work to make up for it. Note that the table itself holds one number
for each of the 5,307 cells; the store counts only beam nodes.

Without the table, the search finished only for \(B\ge32\). Widths 1 to 16 used up the budget of
\(10^7\) expansions, about a second each, still holding their first or second path. The
\(B=16\) search had already found the optimal route, but could not prove it. \(B=32\) needed
8,074 expansions without the table and 4,167 with it.

>> Where the work goes

The re-expansion without the table is easiest to see on the map. We count how often each cell
is expanded by beam-stack search with \(B=16\), with and without the table, the latter stopped
after \(10^7\) expansions.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/beam_stack_heat.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto grid = alg::terrain_from_frame(dm::datasets::volcano(), "x", "y", "elevation", 5000.0 / 600.0);
  const std::size_t start = 80 * grid.cols + 5, goal = 5 * grid.cols + 55;
  const double inf = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < grid.rows; ++i) xs.push_back(grid.cell_size * i);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.cols; ++j) ys.push_back(grid.cell_size * j);

  std::print("| finished-cell table | expansions | cells expanded at least once | most expansions of one cell |\n"
             "| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  for (const bool table : {false, true}) {
    const auto r = alg::terrain_beam_stack_search(grid, start, goal, alg::TerrainHeuristic::OctileClimb, 16, inf,
                                                  table, 10'000'000);
    std::vector<double> count(grid.elevation.size(), 0.0);
    for (const std::size_t c : r.best.expansion_order) count[c] += 1.0;
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(grid.rows, std::vector<double>(grid.cols));
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < count.size(); ++c)
      z[c / grid.cols][c % grid.cols] = count[c] > 0 ? std::log10(count[c]) : std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN();
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.0f} |\n", table ? "yes" : "no", r.best.expansion_order.size(),
               std::ranges::count_if(count, [](double c) { return c > 0; }), std::ranges::max(count));
    std::vector<double> px, py;
    for (const std::size_t c : r.best.path) {
      px.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c / grid.cols));
      py.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c % grid.cols));
    }
    layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                   .image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::terrain_colors(12), 0.0, 6.0)
                   .lines(px, py, "", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 2.0)
                   .x_limits(0, 860)
                   .y_limits(0, 600)
                   .title(table ? "B = 16 with the table: log10 expansions per cell"
                                : "B = 16 without the table (stopped at 10^7)")
                   .x_label("x (m)")
                   .y_label("y (m)"));
  }
  layout.size(1200, 470).save_svg("build/beam_stack_heat.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| finished-cell table | expansions | cells expanded at least once | most expansions of one cell |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| no | 10000007 | 1843 | 83392 |
| yes | 8213 | 1947 | 21 |
\image(figures/beam_stack_heat.svg)
// result_end

\caption(How often each cell was expanded, on a log scale from 1 (green) to \(10^6\) (white), by beam-stack search with \(B=16\) without (left) and with (right) the finished-cell table. Blank cells were never expanded. The black line is the best path found.)
\alttext(Two maps of the same 860 by 600 metre area. Coloured cells form a band from the start at the bottom right north to about y equals 300, then north-west to the goal at the top left, plus a second band west along the south to about x equals 380. The black optimal path follows the northern band. Left, without the table: most of the band is yellow to pink, meaning thousands to tens of thousands of expansions per cell, with the most intense pink in the north-east part and the southern band. Right, with the table: the band is nearly all dark green and light green, meaning one to about twenty expansions per cell.)

Both runs explore roughly the same region: the northern route and a band south of the cone,
about 1,850–1,950 cells. Without the table, the \(10^7\) expansions are spread over only 1,843
cells, so the average cell was expanded over 5,000 times and one cell 83,392 times. Each
backtrack regenerates a layer from scratch, and every cell below it is rediscovered along a new
combination of earlier slices. With the table, a cell is expanded at most 21 times, once per
strictly cheaper route found to it, and the whole search needs 8,213 expansions.

> Summary

- Beam-stack search stores, for each layer, the range of \(f\) still to explore, and backtracks
  by moving the deepest cut layer on to its next slice. It is complete and, with an admissible
  heuristic, optimal, while holding at most \(B\) nodes per layer, at most \(B(U/c_{\min}+1)\) in all.
- `algorithms::terrain_beam_stack_search()` found and proved the optimal route on Maunga Whau for
  every width from 1 to 128, where plain beam search failed for \(B\le2\) and was 25% off for
  \(B=4\) and 8.
- Forgetting dropped layers makes narrow beams re-expand cells exponentially often. The optional
  finished-cell table prunes dominated nodes, keeps the guarantee and brings every width under
  205,000 expansions, at the cost of one number per cell.
- The width trades memory against time: \(B=1\) held 121 nodes but needed 155,657 expansions;
  \(B=32\) held 3,003 and needed 4,167.

> References

- Zhou, R. and Hansen, E. A. (2005). Beam-stack search: integrating backtracking with beam
  search. *Proceedings of the 15th International Conference on Automated Planning and
  Scheduling (ICAPS 2005)*, 90–98.
- Zhang, W. (1998). Complete anytime beam search. *Proceedings of AAAI-98*, 425–430.
- Reinefeld, A. and Marsland, T. A. (1994). Enhanced iterative-deepening search. *IEEE
  Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence* 16(7), 701–710 (transposition tables
  in bounded-memory search).
